      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We start by notating the matrix expression as the product of matrices:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nTaking the determinant of each matrix in the product, we find:\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\dots,\\]\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 1.\\]\nThe determinant of a product of matrices is the product of the determinants of each individual matrix. Therefore,\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\dotsm 1 = 1.\\]\nWe can also take the inverse of each matrix in the product, one by one, and notice that:\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\dots,\\]\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = 1.\\]\nThe determinant of the inverse of a matrix is the reciprocal of the determinant of the matrix. Therefore,\n\\[\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} \\dotsm \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1} \\dotsm \\frac{1}{1} = 1.\\]\nTherefore, the product of all the matrices in the product is the identity matrix. Hence, we have:\n\\[\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\\]",
