      "text": "We observe that when the scalars add up to $100,$  the final result is the identity matrix. Since the problem involves multiple matrices, where each matrix is exactly paired unconditionally to another such matrix, we need to determine which pairs we will be multiplying ourselves. All of the matrices have one row equal to zero and the other has $1$, which means that when we pair them together, we will always end up with the identity matrix. Hence the answer is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}$. The answer is \\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}",
