      "text": "To find the product of the matrices \\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\] we first need to understand the effect of multiplying two such matrices of the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) on a general matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ 0 & d \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's consider the product of two matrices \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & p \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & p \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k \\cdot 1 + k \\cdot 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k + k \\\\ 0 & 1 + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo, the product of any two matrices \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & m \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2k + 2m \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the product of the eight matrices:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 98 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe product of all these matrices can be written as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 + 2 + 4 + 6 + \\dots + 98 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The sum of the harmonics can be simplified using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\\[\n2 + 2 + 4 + 6 + \\dots + 98 = 2 (1 + 2 + 3 + \\dots + 49) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{49 \\cdot 50}{2} = 49 \\cdot 50 = 2450.\n\\]\nSo, the product is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2450 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\cdot 1 + 2450 \\cdot 0 & 1 \\cdot 99 + 2450 \\cdot 2 \\\\ 0 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 & 0 \\cdot 99 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 + 4900 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4999 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4999 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
