      "text": "Let's solve the given problem step-by-step. We need to determine the product of the matrices:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's look at the product of two such matrices:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\cdot 1 + a \\cdot 0 & 1 \\cdot b + a \\cdot 1 \\\\ 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 0 \\cdot b + 1 \\cdot 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b + a \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo, we can generalize this to:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a + b \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis pattern holds for all consecutive matrices in the sequence. Therefore, the product of matrices in the sequence is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 + 3 + 5 + \\dots + 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the arithmetic series \\(1 + 3 + 5 + \\dots + 99\\). This is an arithmetic series where the first term \\(a = 1\\), the common difference \\(d = 2\\), and the last term \\(l = 99\\).\n\nThe sum of an arithmetic series \\(S_n\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(n\\) is the number of terms. First, we need to find \\(n\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic series is given by:\n\n\\[\nl = a + (n-1)d.\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the values \\(l = 99\\), \\(a = 1\\), and \\(d = 2\\):\n\n\\[\n99 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 \\implies 99 = 1 + 2n - 2 \\implies 99 = 2n - 1 \\implies 100 = 2n \\implies n = 50.\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 50 terms in the series. Now, we can find the sum of the series:\n\n\\[\nS_{50} = \\frac{50}{2} (1 + 99) = 25 \\cdot 100 = 2500.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2500 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
